= Functional programming, data structures and algorithms, procedural programming, and digital hardware
{scope}
{title2=CCOM 4213}

Original examination: <Functional programming, data structures and algorithms, procedural programming, and digital hardware examination paper by the University of Oxford (2005)>.

== 1
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

The <map function> transforms each head and recursively processes the tail. A <right fold> combines a head with the result for its tail, whereas a <left fold> passes an updated accumulator into the recursive call. In <Haskell>:

```
import Prelude hiding (map, foldr, foldl, concat)

map f [] = []
map f (x:xs) = f x : map f xs

foldr op e [] = e
foldr op e (x:xs) = op x (foldr op e xs)

foldl op e [] = e
foldl op e (x:xs) = foldl op (op e x) xs

concat = foldr (++) []
```

The last <right fold> uses <list concatenation> to replace each outer cons by appending its inner <list>. Thus `concat [xs,ys,zs] = xs ++ (ys ++ (zs ++ []))`. Its <right fold> association matters: appending a <list> traverses its left spine, so these equations traverse each inner spine once on finite inputs. The concise answer is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{concat}=\operatorname{foldr}\ (\mathbin{++})\ [].}
$$

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Let $P(zss)$ mean `concat (map f zss) = f (concat zss)`. We use <structural induction on lazy lists>, including the <bottom value> and allowing arbitrary, possibly partial, inner <lists>.

For `[]`, the left side is `[]` and the right side is `f [] = []`. For the <bottom value> $\bot$, both `map f` and `concat` are <strict functions> in the outer spine, and the supplied strictness of $f$ makes the right side $f\,\bot=\bot$ too.

For the cons case, assume $P(zss)$ and write the new head as `xs`. The defining equations and the <induction hypothesis> give
$$
\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{concat}(\operatorname{map}f\,(xs:zss))
&=f(xs)\mathbin{++}\operatorname{concat}(\operatorname{map}f\,zss)\\
&=f(xs)\mathbin{++}f(\operatorname{concat}zss)\\
&=f(xs\mathbin{++}\operatorname{concat}zss)\\
&=f(\operatorname{concat}(xs:zss)).
\end{aligned}
$$
The third equality is exactly the supplied <list concatenation> preservation law. No inspection of `xs` was required, so the argument includes partial inner <lists> and undefined elements.

This proves the identity for every finite outer spine ending in either `[]` or $\bot$. In the usual pure <Haskell> semantics, these <functions> are <Scott continuous maps>. An infinite outer <list> is obtained as a limit by <finite approximation of a lazy list>; both composite <functions> preserve that limit. Equality on all its finite approximations therefore gives equality on the infinite outer <list>. Equivalently, $P$ is an <admissible predicate on lazy lists>. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{concat}\circ\operatorname{map}f=f\circ\operatorname{concat}.}
$$
The continuity assumption is the usual semantic assumption for definable <functions> here; an extension from finite to infinite inputs cannot be justified by ordinary finite <induction> alone.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Use <function extensionality>: show $f(x)=g(x)$ for every value $x$ of the argument type. In <lazy evaluation> semantics, “every value” includes the <bottom value> and partial values. For <list> arguments, <structural induction on lazy lists> supplies the empty, bottom and cons cases, with continuity justifying passage to infinite <lists>.

The original printed equation has the same <right fold> on both sides, including the same operator and seed; this is visible in the scan.<Functional programming, data structures and algorithms, procedural programming, and digital hardware examination paper by the University of Oxford (2005)>[Printed p. 2, Question 1(c), displayed equation]{ref} Thus its literal answer is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{foldr}\ (\oplus)\ e=\operatorname{foldr}\ (\oplus)\ e
\quad\text{whenever the expression is well typed}.}
$$
For every input $xs$, both sides denote the very same expression `foldr (oplus) e xs`; equality follows by reflexivity. This remains true if its value is $\bot$. Neither termination nor <associativity> nor an identity law is required. The scan does not establish a different intended equation, so no replacement by a <left fold> is assumed.

== 2
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Let $m,n$ be the two input lengths and suppose numeric comparisons cost constant time. The <list-based set representation> determines which <representation invariant> each answer must preserve.

For unsorted <lists> allowing repetition, retain every left-hand occurrence whose value appears on the right for <set intersection>, and use <list concatenation> for <set union>:

```
interR xs ys = [x | x <- xs, x `elem` ys]
unionR xs ys = xs ++ ys
```

The resulting <lists> represent exactly the desired <sets>; repeated entries are permitted. Membership by <sequential search> makes `interR` take $O(mn)$ <time complexity> in the worst case. The <list concatenation> in `unionR` costs $O(m)$ to build its copied left spine and shares `ys`; fully enumerating the resulting <list> costs $O(m+n)$.

For unsorted, duplicate-free inputs, the same intersection keeps uniqueness, while the union retains `xs` and appends only right-hand elements absent from `xs`:

```
interU xs ys = [x | x <- xs, x `elem` ys]
unionU xs ys = xs ++ [y | y <- ys, not (y `elem` xs)]
```

Neither filtered <list> has internal repetitions, and the filter in `unionU` also excludes overlap with `xs`. Both operations have worst-case $O(mn+m+n)$ <time complexity>, customarily written $O(mn)$ when both lengths are positive. Using the original `xs` for each membership test avoids searching an ever-growing output.

For sorted, duplicate-free <lists>, compare only the two current heads:

```
interS [] ys = []
interS xs [] = []
interS (x:xs) (y:ys)
  | x < y = interS xs (y:ys)
  | x > y = interS (x:xs) ys
  | otherwise = x : interS xs ys

unionS [] ys = ys
unionS xs [] = xs
unionS (x:xs) (y:ys)
  | x < y = x : unionS xs (y:ys)
  | x > y = y : unionS (x:xs) ys
  | otherwise = x : unionS xs ys
```

When $x<y$, sortedness proves that $x$ cannot occur anywhere in the right <list>, so it can be discarded from the <set intersection> or immediately emitted to the <set union>. The symmetric argument handles $y<x$; equal heads are consumed together. Each step consumes at least one input head, so both have $O(m+n)$ worst-case <time complexity>. These bounds assume the sorted <representation invariant> is already established; sorting arbitrary inputs has its own cost.
\Table[
|| Representation
|| <Set intersection>
|| <Set union>, including output traversal

| Unsorted, repetitions allowed
| $O(mn+m+n)$
| $O(m+n)$

| Unsorted, unique
| $O(mn+m+n)$
| $O(mn+m+n)$

| Sorted, unique
| $O(m+n)$
| $O(m+n)$
]

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Use <permutation generation by insertion>: recursively arrange the tail, then insert the removed head in every possible position.

```
inserts x [] = [[x]]
inserts x (y:ys) = (x:y:ys) : map (y:) (inserts x ys)

perms [] = [[]]
perms (x:xs) = concat (map (inserts x) (perms xs))
```

The <map function> in `inserts` prefixes `y` to each placement after the first position. For a <list> of length $k$, the number of results obeys $I(0)=1$ and $I(k+1)=1+I(k)$, so <mathematical induction> gives $I(k)=k+1$. Every result still contains the same $k$ old elements and the one inserted element.

Let $P(n)$ be the length of `perms xs` for an input of length $n$. There is exactly one <permutation> of the empty <list>, namely the empty <list> itself: hence `perms []` is `[[]]`, with length one. For a nonempty input, each recursively obtained <permutation> has length $n-1$ and gives $n$ placements. Thus
$$
P(0)=1=0!,\qquad P(n)=nP(n-1),\qquad
\boxed{P(n)=n!.}
$$
Length alone would not exclude repeated answers. Since the input elements are distinct, removing the distinguished element $x$ from any result uniquely recovers both its placement and the shorter <permutation>. Hence different construction choices give different outputs, and every desired <permutation> is obtained by inserting $x$ into its restriction to `xs`. For `[a,b,c]`, this code yields `[a,b,c]`, `[b,a,c]`, `[b,c,a]`, `[a,c,b]`, `[c,a,b]`, `[c,b,a]`, each exactly once.

== 3
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

To agree with the example, preserve the first occurrence of each value. This <first-occurrence duplicate removal> is a <right fold>:

```
remalldups = foldr step []
  where step x ys = x : filter (/= x) ys
```

The folded tail has no repetitions by the <induction hypothesis>. The <list filter> removes any occurrence of the new head from that tail; prepending the head then gives exactly one occurrence of it, in its first input position. Other values retain their relative order. For the supplied example the intermediate folded results, from right to left, are `[1]`, `[3,1]`, `[2,3,1]`, and `[1,2,3]`. Thus
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{remalldups}[1,2,3,1]=[1,2,3].}
$$
Under <lazy evaluation>, constructing `x : ...` does not first demand the folded tail. That observation is essential for the infinite cases below. The alternative step that first asks whether `x` belongs to the folded tail would lose this property and would also keep last occurrences instead of matching the stated example.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Assume a <proper lazy list> with defined elements and terminating <equality tests>. If infinitely many distinct values occur, <first-occurrence duplicate removal> produces an infinite <list> of distinct values, in first-occurrence order. To produce the next new value after $k$ have appeared, the nested <list filters> need skip only a finite input prefix: an infinite collection of distinct values cannot all belong to the $k$ already seen. The next new value therefore occurs at a finite position and is reached in finite work. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\text{infinitely many distinct values}\ \Longrightarrow\ \text{productive infinite output}.}
$$
This is a <stream productivity> claim, not a bounded-delay claim: arbitrarily long runs of repetitions can make successive delays arbitrarily large. Undefined elements or diverging <equality tests> invalidate the stated assumptions.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Each of the finitely many distinct values has a first occurrence at a finite position, so every one is eventually emitted. After the last new value, however, the <list filters> keep searching the remaining infinite input for another new value. They never observe a terminating `[]`. Therefore the result has a finite visible prefix followed by the <bottom value>:
$$
\boxed{x_1:x_2:\cdots:x_k:\bot,\qquad\text{rather than }[x_1,\ldots,x_k].}
$$
For example, `remalldups (repeat 1)` exposes `1 : ...`, but demanding its tail diverges. This is the <absence-detection obstruction for infinite lists>: no finite inspection can certify that an unseen new value will never occur. The emitted values are correct, but computing the output length or proving the output empty after its last value does not terminate.

=== d
{scope}

==== Solution

<List concatenation> exhausts one inner <list> before reaching the next. For example, with rows `[0,2..]` and `[1]`, `concat` outputs the infinite even row and never reaches `1`. <First-occurrence duplicate removal> cannot recover a value that its input stream never supplies. Hence
$$
\boxed{\text{ordinary concatenation does not give a fair enumeration of infinite rows}.}
$$
The obstruction is separate from the undefined final tail in part (c): here a required distinct value is never emitted at all. A <fair enumeration> must give every row and every element within it a finite opportunity to contribute.

=== e
{scope}

==== Solution

For two <proper lazy lists>, `twiddle` alternates heads, swapping which <list> is served after each output. Thus both input positions have finite waiting time. A tempting extension is `remalldups . foldr twiddle []`, but arbitrary empty rows make that insufficient: for `[ [1,2] ] ++ repeat []`, it emits `1` and then demands the combination of infinitely many empty rows before it can return to `2`.

Use <diagonal merging of lazy lists> to avoid that obstruction. Maintain a finite active collection, add at most one new row per round, and take one head from every nonempty active row. The supplied `twiddle` combines those singleton contributions into a finite batch:

```
union' = remalldups . rounds []

rounds [] [] = []
rounds active [] = emit active []
rounds active (row:rows) = emit (active ++ [row]) rows

emit active rows = batch ++ rounds tails rows
  where (batch, tails) = sweep active

sweep [] = ([], [])
sweep ([]:rows) = sweep rows
sweep ((x:xs):rows) = (twiddle [x] heads, xs:tails)
  where (heads, tails) = sweep rows
```

Here `rounds` takes the finite active collection first and the not-yet-introduced outer <list> second. Because `heads` is a finite <list>, `twiddle [x] heads` emits `x` followed by those heads; the finite batch is then completely consumed before the next round. Empty exhausted rows are removed by `sweep`. No step tries to combine infinitely many not-yet-introduced rows.

Index rows and positions from zero. Row $i$ is introduced in round $i$ and contributes its element $j$ in round $i+j$, if that element exists. Every earlier round inspects only finitely many rows. Therefore every occurrence has a finite emission time, proving <fair enumeration>. Applying <first-occurrence duplicate removal> gives
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{union}'=\operatorname{remalldups}\circ\text{finite-round diagonal enumeration}.}
$$
All outer and inner spines must be <proper lazy lists> and <equality tests> must terminate. Infinitely many empty rows after the final contribution still prevent certifying a terminating empty tail, just as part (c) predicts. Undefined input spines have the separate <partial-list interleaving obstruction>; the construction makes no promise to bypass them.

=== f
{scope}

==== Solution

On two finite total <lists>, the equations agree. If either <list> is empty they return the other; if both have heads, expanding `twiddle` twice gives `x : y : twiddle xs ys`, exactly the third equation for `twiddle'`. The same finite-prefix reasoning proves agreement on infinite inputs with defined spines.

They differ on <partial lists>. Take the first argument `[1]` and the second argument $\bot$. The original definition can emit its first head immediately:
$$
\operatorname{twiddle}\ [1]\ \bot
=1:\operatorname{twiddle}\ \bot\ []
=1:\bot.
$$
After recognizing the nonempty first argument, `twiddle'` must inspect the second argument to distinguish its second and third equations. That inspection diverges, so
$$
\operatorname{twiddle}'\ [1]\ \bot=\bot,\qquad
\boxed{\operatorname{twiddle}'\ne\operatorname{twiddle}.}
$$
For example, demanding only the head distinguishes them: the original returns `1`, while the primed version diverges. Equality of <functions> under <lazy evaluation> requires agreement on these partial inputs too.

== 4
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

A <divide-and-conquer algorithm> splits an instance into smaller instances of the same kind, solves them recursively, and combines their answers. The smaller instances should be sufficiently smaller to ensure <algorithm termination>; the combination cost determines whether splitting saves work. The two halves of <merge sort> illustrate this separation.

<Dynamic programming> identifies a collection of subproblems and dependencies among them, solves each distinct subproblem once, and stores its answer. A table in dependency order or <memoization> prevents repeated evaluation of overlapping subproblems. For optimization, an <optimal substructure> argument justifies constructing an optimal answer from optimal smaller answers.

A <greedy algorithm> repeatedly makes a locally best feasible choice and commits to it. It avoids exploring alternative continuations. <Algorithm correctness> therefore needs a <greedy-choice property>, often proved by replacing the first choice of an optimal solution with the greedy choice without worsening it.
$$
\boxed{\text{divide and combine; reuse subproblem answers; commit to safe local choices}.}
$$

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

<Divide-and-conquer algorithms> suit problems with largely independent, balanced subproblems and an inexpensive combination step. In <merge sort>, the two halves are independently sorted and merged in linear time. Its <recurrence relation> is $T(n)=T(\lfloor n/2\rfloor)+T(\lceil n/2\rceil)+\Theta(n)$, giving $\Theta(n\log n)$ <time complexity>.

<Dynamic programming> suits overlapping subproblems with a compact state description. For the <coin change problem>, let $C(v)$ be the minimum number of coins needed to make amount $v$. With positive denominations $D$ containing $1$,
$$
C(0)=0,\qquad C(v)=1+\min_{d\in D,\ d\leq v}C(v-d).
$$
Every optimal nonempty answer has a last coin $d$ and an optimal answer for $v-d$; otherwise replacing the remainder improves it. Filling $C(0),\ldots,C(V)$ takes $O(V|D|)$ <time complexity> and $O(V)$ <space complexity>. Repeated amounts are computed just once. This bound is in the numeric amount $V$, not its binary input length.

A <greedy algorithm> suits problems where one can prove a <greedy-choice property>. For unweighted <interval scheduling>, choose an interval with earliest finishing time, discard overlapping intervals, and repeat. Among optimal schedules, replace the first interval by the earliest-finishing one. The replacement finishes no later, so every later interval remains feasible. Applying the same argument to the remaining intervals proves optimality. After sorting by finish time, a single scan suffices.

These examples separate the relevant structural reasons: independent halves, repeated amount states, and an exchange argument certifying each irreversible choice.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

In the <coin change problem>, take denominations $\{1,3,4\}$ and target amount $6$. The natural <greedy algorithm> repeatedly chooses the largest denomination not exceeding the remaining amount. It chooses $4$, then $1$, then $1$, for three coins. Yet $3+3=6$ uses only two.

Two is optimal: one coin cannot make $6$ because denomination $6$ is absent, and the displayed pair attains the lower bound. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{greedy: }4+1+1\ (3\text{ coins});\qquad
\text{optimal: }3+3\ (2\text{ coins}).}
$$
The locally largest first coin loses the possibility of using the better pair of threes. This supplies a concrete failure of the <greedy-choice property>; <optimal substructure> alone does not justify the greedy choice. The <dynamic programming> recurrence from part (b) would compare all feasible first or last denominations and find the pair.

== 5
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

For <heapsort>, use the usual <array> representation of a <max-heap>, indexed from zero, with children $2i+1,2i+2$. Build the <max-heap> from the last internal node towards the root, using <heapify down>. The three build stages are

```
start                    [2,7,3,2,8,1]
sift at index 2          [2,7,3,2,8,1]
sift at index 1          [2,8,3,2,7,1]
sift at index 0          [8,7,3,2,2,1]
```

At index zero, the value `2` moves below `8` and then below `7`. Next exchange the root with the last active element, shorten the active <heap>, and restore <heap order>. A vertical separator below distinguishes the active prefix from its sorted final suffix.

\Table[
|| Removed maximum
|| After root exchange
|| After restoring <heap order>

| $8$
| `1,7,3,2,2 | 8`
| `7,2,3,1,2 | 8`

| $7$
| `2,2,3,1 | 7,8`
| `3,2,2,1 | 7,8`

| $3$
| `1,2,2 | 3,7,8`
| `2,1,2 | 3,7,8`

| $2$
| `2,1 | 2,3,7,8`
| `2,1 | 2,3,7,8`

| $2$
| `1 | 2,2,3,7,8`
| `1 | 2,2,3,7,8`
]

The trace chooses the left child when equal children tie; other tie conventions can give different intermediate <heaps> with the same sorted output.

For <quicksort>, use the head as pivot and partition the remaining elements into those strictly less than it and those greater than or equal to it, retaining their order within each partition. This specifies how the duplicate `2` is handled:

```
pivot 2:  [1]       | 2 | [7,3,2,8]
pivot 7:  [3,2]     | 7 | [8]
pivot 3:  [2]       | 3 | []
```

Empty and singleton <lists> are already sorted. Returning from the recursive calls gives

```
Q [3,2]       = [2] ++ [3] ++ []       = [2,3]
Q [7,3,2,8]   = [2,3] ++ [7] ++ [8]   = [2,3,7,8]
Q [2,7,3,2,8,1] = [1] ++ [2] ++ [2,3,7,8]
```

For <counting sort>, count keys $1$ through $8$. After reading each input key, the count vector is

```
initial: (0,0,0,0,0,0,0,0)
read 2:  (0,1,0,0,0,0,0,0)
read 7:  (0,1,0,0,0,0,1,0)
read 3:  (0,1,1,0,0,0,1,0)
read 2:  (0,2,1,0,0,0,1,0)
read 8:  (0,2,1,0,0,0,1,1)
read 1:  (1,2,1,0,0,0,1,1)
```

The cumulative counts are $(1,3,4,4,4,4,5,6)$. For the usual <stable sorting> version with zero-based output indices, scan the input from right to left, place a key $k$ at position $C[k]-1$, and decrement $C[k]$. The successive placements are `1` at $0$, `8` at $5$, the later `2` at $2$, `3` at $3$, `7` at $4$, and the earlier `2` at $1$. The final answer for all three <sorting algorithms> is
$$
\boxed{[1,2,2,3,7,8].}
$$

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

<Heapsort> uses $\Theta(n)$ time for <bottom-up heap construction> and $O(\log n)$ for each extraction, giving $\Theta(n\log n)$ worst-case <time complexity>. An iterative <heapify down> on an <array> uses $O(1)$ auxiliary <space complexity>. Ordinary <heapsort> is not a <stable sorting> method.

<Quicksort> has balanced-case and average-case $\Theta(n\log n)$ <time complexity> when the input order supplies suitably distributed pivot ranks. With the prescribed first-element pivot, sorted inputs produce subproblems of sizes $0$ and $n-1$, giving $T(n)=T(n-1)+\Theta(n)=\Theta(n^2)$. With the stated two-way partition convention, all-equal keys also give this worst case. There is no input-independent expected bound without a distribution or randomization assumption. For an in-place <array> implementation, the recursion stack is $O(\log n)$ when balanced and $O(n)$ in the worst case; the functional <list> trace also allocates new partition spines and copied concatenation prefixes. Ordinary in-place <quicksort> is not <stable sorting>; the stable-partition <list> convention used in the trace can preserve equal-key order at additional allocation cost.

<Counting sort> uses $\Theta(n+k)$ <time complexity> for $k$ possible consecutive <integer> keys. The <stable sorting> version uses $\Theta(n+k)$ auxiliary <space complexity> including the output <array>; counting and re-emitting bare keys needs only $O(k)$ beyond its output. It is attractive when $k$ is small, but a sparse huge key range makes both initialization and count storage expensive. It relies on indexing by bounded <integer> keys, so it is not subject to the <comparison sorting lower bound>.
$$
\boxed{\text{heapsort: }\Theta(n\log n);\quad
\text{quicksort worst case: }\Theta(n^2);\quad
\text{counting sort: }\Theta(n+k).}
$$

== 6
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

An <Oberon> <recursive procedure> can implement the defining <recurrence relation> directly. Its <precondition> is $n\geq1$; arithmetic is assumed to remain in the representable <integer> range.

```
PROCEDURE Fib(n: INTEGER): INTEGER;
  VAR result: INTEGER;
BEGIN
  IF n <= 2 THEN
    result := 1
  ELSE
    result := Fib(n-1) + Fib(n-2)
  END;
  RETURN result
END Fib;
```

Correctness follows by <strong induction>: the two base inputs give $1$, and for $n>2$ the returned sum is $F_{n-1}+F_{n-2}=F_n$. On each recursive branch the positive <integer> argument strictly decreases, while recursion stops at $1$ or $2$. The <well-ordering principle> therefore proves <algorithm termination>.
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Fib}(n)=F_n\quad(n\geq1).}
$$
The method repeats many subproblems and has exponential <time complexity>; termination alone does not make it efficient.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

In <tail recursion>, each recursive call is a <tail call>: its result is returned directly, with no suspended addition or other work. A compiler can then reuse an activation record, giving constant stack use. This is a possible optimization, not a guarantee made by the source syntax. The direct <Fibonacci number> procedure in part (a) must retain information while waiting to add two recursive results.

Extend the <Fibonacci numbers> with $F_0=0$ and use the suggested quantity $g(n,a,b)=aF_n+bF_{n-1}$. For $n>1$,
$$
aF_n+bF_{n-1}=(a+b)F_{n-1}+aF_{n-2}
=g(n-1,a+b,a),
$$
while $g(1,a,b)=a$. This gives the <Oberon> procedures

```
PROCEDURE G(n, a, b: INTEGER): INTEGER;
  VAR result: INTEGER;
BEGIN
  IF n = 1 THEN
    result := a
  ELSE
    result := G(n-1, a+b, a)
  END;
  RETURN result
END G;

PROCEDURE FibTail(n: INTEGER): INTEGER;
BEGIN
  RETURN G(n, 1, 0)
END FibTail;
```

The assignment of the recursive result does not perform arithmetic after the call; it merely passes that result to `RETURN`, so the call is in <tail recursion>. A dialect permitting an immediate `RETURN G(...)` in the branch expresses the same control flow. The decreasing argument $n-1$ proves <algorithm termination>, and the displayed identity proves correctness by <mathematical induction>. Thus
$$
\boxed{F_n=g(n,1,0),\qquad g(n,a,b)=g(n-1,a+b,a)\ (n>1).}
$$
There are $n-1$ recursive steps, giving $O(n)$ arithmetic operations. Stack use is $O(1)$ with <tail call> elimination and $O(n)$ without it. With unbounded <integers>, the growing <Fibonacci numbers> also make arithmetic costs grow; the linear bound counts arithmetic operations.

=== c
{scope}

==== Solution

Replace the recursive transfer in part (b) by simultaneous state updates, using a temporary variable to preserve the old `a`:

```
PROCEDURE FibIter(n: INTEGER): INTEGER;
  VAR k, a, b, oldA: INTEGER;
BEGIN
  k := n; a := 1; b := 0;
  WHILE k > 1 DO
    oldA := a;
    a := a + b;
    b := oldA;
    k := k - 1
  END;
  RETURN a
END FibIter;
```

For original input $N\geq1$, a <loop invariant> is
$$
\boxed{1\leq k\leq N,\qquad aF_k+bF_{k-1}=F_N.}
$$
It holds initially because $a=1,b=0,k=N$. Each iteration preserves it by the identity from part (b). The <loop variant> is $k-1$, a nonnegative <integer> that decreases by one whenever the guard holds. On exit $k=1$, so the <loop invariant> gives $aF_1+bF_0=a=F_N$, exactly the returned answer. The procedure uses $O(n)$ arithmetic operations and $O(1)$ stored <integer> variables, without depending on compiler treatment of <tail calls>.

== 7
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Represent the two recursively split <arrays> by adjacent half-open ranges of the original <array>. This avoids allocating a fresh pair at every call. The caller supplies a distinct work <array> with at least as many elements as `a`; only that work <array> is used for merging. The <Oberon> code is

```
PROCEDURE SortRange(VAR a, work: ARRAY OF INTEGER;
                    l, r: INTEGER);
  VAR m, i, j, k: INTEGER;
BEGIN
  IF r-l > 1 THEN
    m := l + (r-l) DIV 2;
    SortRange(a, work, l, m);
    SortRange(a, work, m, r);
    i := l; j := m; k := l;
    WHILE (i < m) & (j < r) DO
      IF a[i] <= a[j] THEN
        work[k] := a[i]; i := i+1
      ELSE
        work[k] := a[j]; j := j+1
      END;
      k := k+1
    END;
    WHILE i < m DO
      work[k] := a[i]; i := i+1; k := k+1
    END;
    WHILE j < r DO
      work[k] := a[j]; j := j+1; k := k+1
    END;
    k := l;
    WHILE k < r DO
      a[k] := work[k]; k := k+1
    END
  END
END SortRange;

PROCEDURE MergeSort(VAR a, work: ARRAY OF INTEGER);
BEGIN
  SortRange(a, work, 0, LEN(a))
END MergeSort;
```

For a range of length $n>1$, the left length is $p=\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ and the right length is $q=\lceil n/2\rceil$, so $p\leq q\leq p+1$ as required. Both are positive and smaller than $n$, giving the recursive <loop variant> analogue $r-l$ for <algorithm termination>. Empty ranges are also harmless, although the question supplies a nonempty <array>.

For the first merge loop, regard the two sorted ranges as fixed input sequences $B=a[l:m)$ and $C=a[m:r)$. They are not overwritten during the merge. A <loop invariant> is that $l\leq i\leq m$, $m\leq j\leq r$, $k-l=(i-l)+(j-m)$, and `work[l:k)` is the stable sorted merge of the consumed prefixes `a[l:i)` and `a[m:j)`. Every emitted element is at most every remaining element. Taking the smaller head preserves this property, and choosing the left head on equality preserves <stable sorting>. The <loop variant> is
$$
(m-i)+(r-j)=r-k.
$$
It is nonnegative and decreases by one per iteration. On exit at least one range is exhausted.

For the second loop, the remaining left range is already sorted and any right remainder is absent whenever that loop runs. Appending the next left element preserves the same merge <loop invariant>; its <loop variant> is $m-i$. For the third loop, the left range is exhausted and the right remainder is appended in order; its <loop variant> is $r-j$. On completing both loops, `work[l:r)` is the complete sorted merge and $k=r$.

In the final copying loop, the <loop invariant> is that `a[l:k)` equals `work[l:k)`, `work[l:r)` remains the correct sorted answer, and positions outside `a[l:r)` are unchanged. Its <loop variant> is $r-k$. At exit the whole answer has been copied back. By <strong induction> on range length, the recursive calls sort their halves, and the merge preserves the original <multiset>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\texttt{MergeSort}\text{ returns a sorted permutation of its input}.}
$$
The work <array> must not alias `a`: overwriting the source while merging would invalidate the merge <loop invariant>.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Each merge consumes and copies a total of $n$ elements, so its <time complexity> is $\Theta(n)$. Hence
$$
T(1)=\Theta(1),\qquad
T(n)=T(\lfloor n/2\rfloor)+T(\lceil n/2\rceil)+\Theta(n).
$$
At any level of the recursion tree, all active subranges are disjoint and together contain at most $n$ elements. The total work per level is therefore $O(n)$, and halving gives $O(\log n)$ levels. In fact the full run performs $\Theta(n\log n)$ work for $n\geq2$.

The shared work <array> holds $n$ elements, and the depth-first recursion has $O(\log n)$ simultaneous activation records with a constant number of scalar variables each. No recursive call allocates a fresh <array>. Therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{time }\Theta(n\log n),\qquad
\text{auxiliary space }\Theta(n)+O(\log n)=\Theta(n).}
$$
The work <array> is counted in the auxiliary <space complexity> even though the caller supplies it. If its actual allocated capacity is $M>n$, the reserved storage is $\Theta(M+\log n)$; choosing capacity $n$ gives the stated bound.

== 8
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Use an <Oberon> record containing an active coefficient count and a bounded <array> of coefficients in ascending power order. A count of zero represents the zero <polynomial>; a count $n+1$ represents $a_0+\cdots+a_nx^n$. Trailing zero coefficients are allowed, so the count need not be the <degree of a polynomial> plus one.

```
CONST Capacity = 1024;
TYPE Polynomial = RECORD
  len: INTEGER;
  coeff: ARRAY Capacity OF REAL
END;

PROCEDURE Horner(VAR p: Polynomial; x: REAL): REAL;
  VAR i: INTEGER; value: REAL;
BEGIN
  i := p.len; value := 0.0;
  WHILE i > 0 DO
    i := i-1;
    value := p.coeff[i] + x*value
  END;
  RETURN value
END Horner;
```

The <representation invariant> is $0\leq\texttt{len}\leq\texttt{Capacity}$, with only indices below `len` significant. The parameter is passed by reference to avoid a full record copy; the procedure does not modify it.

Let $L$ be the original coefficient count. At each guard test the <loop invariant> is
$$
0\leq i\leq L,\qquad
v=\sum_{j=i}^{L-1}a_jx^{j-i}.
$$
The empty sum is zero initially. If the old index is $i>0$, after decrementing and updating,
$$
v_{\mathrm{new}}=a_{i-1}+x\sum_{j=i}^{L-1}a_jx^{j-i}
=\sum_{j=i-1}^{L-1}a_jx^{j-(i-1)},
$$
which preserves the <loop invariant>. The <loop variant> is $i$, decreasing by one until zero. At exit,
$$
\boxed{v=\sum_{j=0}^{L-1}a_jx^j=p(x).}
$$
This is exactly the right-to-left nesting of <Horner's method>, matching the supplied <right fold>. It uses $O(L)$ arithmetic operations and $O(1)$ additional scalar storage. `REAL` gives ordinary floating-point evaluation; the displayed identities specify the ideal arithmetic calculation, with the usual rounding in an actual floating-point implementation.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

Add <polynomials> by adding equal-power coefficients, interpreting a missing coefficient as zero. Compute into a local record before assigning the output, so the result may safely be the same actual record as either input.

```
PROCEDURE Add(VAR p, q, result: Polynomial);
  VAR t: Polynomial; i: INTEGER; u, v: REAL;
BEGIN
  t.len := p.len;
  IF q.len > t.len THEN t.len := q.len END;
  i := 0;
  WHILE i < t.len DO
    u := 0.0; v := 0.0;
    IF i < p.len THEN u := p.coeff[i] END;
    IF i < q.len THEN v := q.coeff[i] END;
    t.coeff[i] := u+v;
    i := i+1
  END;
  result := t
END Add;
```

With $L=\max(p.\texttt{len},q.\texttt{len})$, the <loop invariant> is $0\leq i\leq L$ and, for $0\leq k<i$, `t.coeff[k]` is the sum of the two extended input coefficients at $k$. The <loop variant> is $L-i$. The inputs remain unchanged throughout the loop, and at exit every active coefficient is correct. The <time complexity> is $O(L)$ for the active arithmetic; assigning the fixed-capacity record can additionally copy its full capacity.

For the product, each pair of input terms contributes $a_ib_jx^{i+j}$. Initialize the active result to zero, then accumulate these pairwise contributions:

```
PROCEDURE Multiply(VAR p, q, result: Polynomial);
  VAR t: Polynomial; i, j, k: INTEGER;
BEGIN
  t.len := 0;
  IF (p.len > 0) & (q.len > 0) THEN
    t.len := p.len + q.len - 1
  END;
  (* Precondition: t.len <= Capacity. *)
  k := 0;
  WHILE k < t.len DO
    t.coeff[k] := 0.0; k := k+1
  END;
  IF t.len > 0 THEN
    i := 0;
    WHILE i < p.len DO
      j := 0;
      WHILE j < q.len DO
        t.coeff[i+j] := t.coeff[i+j]
                          + p.coeff[i]*q.coeff[j];
        j := j+1
      END;
      i := i+1
    END
  END;
  result := t
END Multiply;
```

The input <representation invariants> and the capacity <precondition> are assumed. No coefficient is accessed when either input is the zero <polynomial>. The initialization <loop invariant> says coefficients below $k$ are zero; its <loop variant> is `t.len-k`.

For positive input lengths $m,n$, the outer <loop invariant> says that $0\leq i\leq m$ and each coefficient $t_k$ equals
$$
\sum_{0\leq u<i}\ \sum_{\substack{0\leq v<n\\u+v=k}}a_ub_v.
$$
The inner <loop invariant> adds the contributions with $u=i$ and $0\leq v<j$ to that sum, while $0\leq j\leq n$. The update adds exactly the next missing term at index $i+j$, preserving the inner <loop invariant>. The <loop variants> are $m-i$ for the outer loop and $n-j$ for the inner loop. On exit all pairs have contributed, yielding
$$
\boxed{(p+q)_k=a_k+b_k,\qquad
(pq)_k=\sum_{i+j=k}a_ib_j.}
$$
The product uses $O(mn+m+n)$ arithmetic operations and a temporary coefficient <array> of capacity `Capacity`. Both operations allow trailing zeros; no extra normalization loop is required. Only the active coefficients are meaningful, so inactive record entries are never read by these operations. As in part (a), floating-point coefficients implement these formulas subject to rounding.

== 9
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

Write $x\uparrow y=\neg(x\land y)$ for a two-input <NAND gate>. Three elementary constructions are
$$
\neg x=x\uparrow x,\qquad
x\land y=(x\uparrow y)\uparrow(x\uparrow y),\qquad
x\lor y=(x\uparrow x)\uparrow(y\uparrow y).
$$
The last identity is <De Morgan's laws>. To see that these operations suffice from first principles, take any finite truth table. For each row whose output is $1$, form the conjunction of input literals that matches exactly that row: use $x_i$ when the row has $x_i=1$, and $\neg x_i$ when it has $x_i=0$. Disjoin all such conjunctions. On any input, precisely its matching row conjunction can be true, so this <disjunctive normal form> equals the desired <Boolean function>.

Replace each negation, conjunction and disjunction by the displayed <NAND gate> constructions, nesting two-input gates for longer conjunctions or disjunctions. Constant outputs are available too: for any input $x$, $x\uparrow(x\uparrow x)=1$, and NANDing that value with itself gives $0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{\text{NAND alone is functionally complete for Boolean functions of positive arity}.}
$$
A zero-input constant circuit additionally needs a supplied constant signal, such as a fixed logic supply. The positive-arity construction already covers constant-valued <Boolean functions> having one or more unused inputs.

For a three-input <CMOS NAND gate>, put three <PMOS transistors> in parallel from the positive supply $V_{DD}$ to the output $Y$, and three <NMOS transistors> in series from $Y$ to ground. Each input controls one device in each network:

\Image[computing-prelims-2005-cmos-nand.png]
{title=Three-input CMOS NAND: parallel PMOS pull-up paths and a series NMOS pull-down path}

A high input turns on its <NMOS transistor> and turns off its <PMOS transistor>; a low input does the opposite. If $A=B=C=1$, every series <NMOS transistor> conducts and every <PMOS transistor> is off, pulling $Y$ low. If any input is zero, its <NMOS transistor> breaks the series path and its <PMOS transistor> provides a path to $V_{DD}$, pulling $Y$ high. Consequently
$$
\boxed{Y=\overline{ABC}.}
$$
For ideal settled inputs, the complementary networks do not simultaneously connect supply to ground. The <CMOS> arrangement gives a driven high or low output in every input case, rather than leaving the output floating.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

The printed word “even” conflicts with the displayed numbers: all ten listed <prime numbers> are odd, and $2$ is the only even <prime number>. The scan confirms that wording.<Functional programming, data structures and algorithms, procedural programming, and digital hardware examination paper by the University of Oxford (2005)>[Printed p. 5, Question 9(b), both occurrences of “even” and the final “including 2”]{ref} We first construct the detector for the explicitly listed numbers, then add $2$ as requested. The literal even-prime detector is also given below.

Let the five input <bits> be $A,B,C,D,E$ with value $16A+8B+4C+2D+E$. Every listed number has $E=1$. For that plane, the four-variable <Karnaugh map> is

\Table[
|| $AB\backslash CD$
|| $00$
|| $01$
|| $11$
|| $10$

| $00$
| $0$
| $1$
| $1$
| $1$

| $01$
| $0$
| $1$
| $0$
| $1$

| $11$
| $0$
| $0$
| $1$
| $1$

| $10$
| $1$
| $1$
| $1$
| $0$
]

The entire $E=0$ plane is zero. Five valid groups in the displayed <Karnaugh map> give the product terms
$$
\overline A\,\overline C D,\quad
\overline A C\overline D,\quad
A\overline B\,\overline C,\quad
ABC,\quad
\overline B D.
$$
Including $E$, their accepted numbers are respectively $\{3,11\}$, $\{5,13\}$, $\{17,19\}$, $\{29,31\}$, and $\{3,7,19,23\}$. Their union is exactly the ten listed numbers. Hence an explicit <Boolean circuit> is: invert $A,B,C,D$ where needed, feed the five products into five <AND gates>, combine those outputs with an <OR gate>, and AND with $E$. Its output is
$$
\boxed{P_{\mathrm{odd}}=E\bigl(
\overline A\,\overline C D\lor
\overline A C\overline D\lor
A\overline B\,\overline C\lor
ABC\lor\overline B D\bigr).}
$$
Any gate with more than two inputs can be replaced by a tree of two-input gates. To include the exceptional <prime number> $2$, detect its exact <binary representation> $00010$ and add one OR branch:
$$
\boxed{P_{\mathrm{all}}=P_{\mathrm{odd}}\lor
\overline A\,\overline B\,\overline C D\overline E.}
$$
If “even prime” is read literally instead of following the listed numbers, the complete first detector is simply
$$
\boxed{P_{\mathrm{even}}=\overline A\,\overline B\,\overline C D\overline E.}
$$
All three formulas classify all $32$ possible inputs, including $0$ and $1$ as nonprime.

== 10
{scope}

=== a
{scope}

==== Solution

A <D flip-flop> stores one <bit>. At its specified active <clock edge>, it samples $D$ and, after its <clock-to-Q delay>, makes $Q$ equal to that sampled value. Between active <clock edges> it retains the stored value. The input must meet the <setup and hold time> requirements around the sampling edge; a level-sensitive latch would have different behavior.

For a finite-state <sequential circuit>, encode the present state in a bank of <D flip-flops>. Feed their outputs $q$ and the external inputs $u$ to <combinational logic> computing the next-state <function> $F(q,u)$, and connect those outputs to the flip-flop $D$ inputs. A common <clock signal> then performs the simultaneous update
$$
\boxed{q^+=F(q,u).}
$$
Output <combinational logic> computes $H(q)$ for a <Moore machine> or $H(q,u)$ for a <Mealy machine>. Since any finite next-state and output table is a <Boolean function>, it has a <Boolean circuit> implementation. Initialization specifies the starting state, and unused state encodings require either a recovery rule or an initialization assumption. The <clock period> must accommodate propagation through the <D flip-flops> and intervening <combinational logic> before the next sampling edge.

=== b
{scope}

==== Solution

A particularly transparent design uses six <D flip-flops> in a <one-hot encoding>. Let $Q_i=1$ mean that face $i$ is displayed. Initialize $Q_1=1$ and $Q_2=\cdots=Q_6=0$, and wire the next-state inputs as a ring:
$$
\boxed{D_1=Q_6,\quad D_2=Q_1,\quad D_3=Q_2,\quad
D_4=Q_3,\quad D_5=Q_4,\quad D_6=Q_5.}
$$
All six <D flip-flops> share the same active <clock edge>. Each pulse moves the single stored $1$ to the next position, giving the <state-transition diagram> cycle $1\to2\to3\to4\to5\to6\to1$. The <representation invariant> “exactly one $Q_i$ is high” holds after initialization and is preserved by rotation. Six <D flip-flops> are more than the three-<bit> theoretical minimum, but their simple wiring gives a complete implementation without requiring that minimum.

\Image[computing-prelims-2005-dice-ring.png]
{title=One-hot ring state register and the six lamp patterns, including the original opposite diagonals on faces 2 and 3}

Decode the seven lamps by their positions, with $T,M,B$ denoting top, middle and bottom, and $L,R$ denoting left and right. Let $L_C$ be the center lamp. In the original figure, face 2 uses top-left and bottom-right, while face 3 uses top-right and bottom-left plus center; the two diagonals must therefore have different controls.<Functional programming, data structures and algorithms, procedural programming, and digital hardware examination paper by the University of Oxford (2005)>[Printed p. 6, Figure 1(b), faces 2 and 3]{ref} The required <OR gate> decoder is
$$
\boxed{\begin{aligned}
L_{TL}=L_{BR}&=Q_2\lor Q_4\lor Q_5\lor Q_6,\\
L_{TR}=L_{BL}&=Q_3\lor Q_4\lor Q_5\lor Q_6,\\
L_{ML}=L_{MR}&=Q_6,\\
L_C&=Q_1\lor Q_3\lor Q_5.
\end{aligned}}
$$
For example, only the center is high in state $Q_1$, the first diagonal is high in state $Q_2$, and in state $Q_6$ both outer columns are high while the center is low. This verifies all six layouts directly.

To implement the switch behavior, use a rapid common <clock signal> and a sampled enable $S$, high while the switch is pressed. Each next-state input is then selected between its advancing value and its own stored value:
$$
\boxed{D_i=(S\land Q_{\mathrm{prev}(i)})\lor
(\overline S\land Q_i),\qquad
\mathrm{prev}(1)=6,\quad\mathrm{prev}(i)=i-1\ (i>1).}
$$
One <multiplexer> per <D flip-flop> implements that selection. While enabled the <electronic dice> advances; otherwise every <D flip-flop> reloads its own value and the display holds. The enable supplied to this synchronous design must be stable at sampling edges. A reset or preset facility must establish the specified one-hot initial state: an all-zero ring does not start itself.

With a rapid <clock signal>, uncertainty in human release timing makes the final face practically unpredictable. The <sequential circuit> itself is deterministic; this observation alone is not a proof that its six outcomes have equal probabilities.
